Feature: Backup and Vacuum Operations on customer_360_raw Table in Databricks

  As a Data Engineer,
  I want to develop a PySpark script to efficiently backup the 'customer_360_raw' Unity Catalog table as compressed, partitioned Parquet files
  and perform a vacuum operation to remove records older than 30 days,
  So that data is safely archived without excessive storage usage and compliance with data retention policy is maintained.

  Background:
    Given the Unity Catalog table "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw" is available
    And the backup volume path is "/Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup"
    And the backup format is "Parquet" with "snappy" compression
    And the required partition column is "state" (assumed to be present in the table schema)
    And the field "updated_at" in the table contains timestamps in "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss" format
    And the script will backup all columns from the source table
    And the current UTC time is "2024-06-30 12:00:00"

  Scenario: Successful backup of customer_360_raw table partitioned by state as compressed Parquet
    Given the Databricks compute cluster is running and PySpark environment is available
    And the service account has "read" access on "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw"
    And the service account has "write" access to "/Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup"
    When the PySpark script reads the entire "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw" table
    And the script writes the data as Parquet files to "/Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup" using "snappy" compression
    And the script partitions the output files by the "state" column
    Then the "/Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup" should contain Parquet files partitioned by each unique state value present in the table
    And all columns from the source table must be present in each Parquet file
    And each Parquet file must be compressed using "snappy"
    And no exception should be raised during the operation

  Scenario Outline: Error during backup due to insufficient permissions
    Given the Databricks compute cluster is running and PySpark environment is available
    And the service account has "<read_permission>" access on "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw"
    And the service account has "<write_permission>" access to "/Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup"
    When the PySpark script attempts to write as Parquet to the backup volume
    Then a "<error_message>" error should be raised

    Examples:
      | read_permission | write_permission | error_message                                     |
      | read           | none             | ACCESS_DENIED: No WRITE privilege on backup volume |
      | none           | write            | ACCESS_DENIED: No READ privilege on source table   |
      | none           | none             | ACCESS_DENIED: No READ or WRITE privileges         |

  Scenario Outline: Error due to missing required table or partition column
    Given the Databricks compute cluster is running and PySpark environment is available
    And the Unity Catalog table "<table_name>" is "<table_state>"
    And the column "state" is "<state_col_status>" in the table schema
    When the PySpark script runs the backup operation
    Then a "<backup_error>" error should be raised

    Examples:
      | table_name                                               | table_state     | state_col_status    | backup_error                                                         |
      | purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw       | missing         | present            | TABLE_NOT_FOUND: customer_360_raw table does not exist               |
      | purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw       | available       | missing            | PARTITION_COLUMN_MISSING: Required partition column 'state' missing  |

  Scenario: Successful vacuum operation to clean records older than 30 days from source table
    Given the Unity Catalog table "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw" contains records with "updated_at" timestamps ranging before and after "2024-05-31 12:00:00"
    And the Databricks session is authorized for "VACUUM" and "DELETE" operations on the table
    When the script executes a vacuum operation to retain only records where "updated_at" >= "2024-05-31 12:00:00"
    Then only records with "updated_at" value on or after "2024-05-31 12:00:00" should remain in the Unity Catalog table
    And all records with "updated_at" earlier than "2024-05-31 12:00:00" must be permanently removed from storage

  Scenario Outline: Vacuum operation error due to faulty datetime format in "updated_at"
    Given the Unity Catalog table contains at least one record with "updated_at" = "<bad_datetime>"
    When the script attempts to filter or vacuum based on "updated_at" for the last 30 days
    Then the script should raise an error with the message "<vacuum_error>"

    Examples:
      | bad_datetime        | vacuum_error                                       |
      | 2024/06/01T12:00Z   | INVALID_DATETIME_FORMAT: 'updated_at' is not yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss |
      | NULL                | NULL_DATETIME_ERROR: 'updated_at' field is null   |

  Scenario: Data consistency check post-backup
    Given the backup Parquet files are present in "/Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup"
    And partitioning by "state" is applied
    When the Parquet files are read back into a DataFrame
    Then the DataFrame row count must be equal to the row count of the Unity Catalog "customer_360_raw" table at the time of backup
    And the column names and their data types must match the source schema exactly
    And no data corruption or loss is allowed

  Scenario Outline: Backup script should handle missing or empty data gracefully
    Given the Unity Catalog table contains "<row_count>" records
    When the backup script is executed
    Then the backup directory "<expected_output>"

    Examples:
      | row_count | expected_output                                                 |
      | 0         | remains empty, and logs "NO_DATA_TO_BACKUP"                    |
      | null      | remains empty, and logs "TABLE_NOT_FOUND_OR_EMPTY"              |

  Scenario: Script logs operations and errors to /dbfs/logs/customer_360_raw_backup.log
    Given the script is executed with logging enabled
    When an operation is performed (backup, vacuum, error)
    Then operation details, timestamps, and exceptions must be appended as a JSON object to "/dbfs/logs/customer_360_raw_backup.log"
    And log entries must include fields: "event_time", "operation", "status", "details", "error_message" (if any)

  Scenario: Parquet backup process must not allow uncompressed output
    When the script is configured with compression codec "none"
    Then the script must raise an error "COMPRESSION_REQUIRED: Output compression must be enabled"
